{"seq":1,"at":"2026-09-17","kind":"imported","target":{"type":"article","id":"a0070"},"reason":"Article of the Quiztopia bank (written for card c006): researched and transcribed in September 2026, converted to the quiz platform on 2026-10-20.","by":"Quiztopia import","ref":"quiz-archive/transcripts/quiztopia/c006.json"},
{"seq":2,"at":"2026-10-20","kind":"edited","target":{"type":"article","id":"a0070"},"reason":"Rewritten by the merge to take in the printed cards' questions.","by":"merge geography/physical","ref":"quiz-archive/runs/tp-ingest/decisions/geography__physical.g2.json","changes":[{"field":"titleEn","old":"Sacred Summits: Five Mountains and the Question of Who May Climb Them","new":"Sacred Summits: Six Mountains and the Question of Who May Climb Them"},{"field":"titleDe","old":"Heilige Gipfel: Fünf Berge und die Frage, wer sie besteigen darf","new":"Heilige Gipfel: Sechs Berge und die Frage, wer sie besteigen darf"},{"field":"bodyEn","diff":[["=",108],["-","Five"],["+","Six"],["=",7],["-","five"],["+","six"],["=",893],["+","In Mexico the answer has been given by the mountain itself. **Popocatépetl**, at about 5,426 metres the country's second-highest peak, rises some 65 to 70 kilometres south-east of Mexico City; its Nahuatl name means \"smoking mountain\", and the Aztecs worshipped it, together with its sister volcano Iztaccíhuatl, as a sacred pair. Its first recorded ascent came in November 1519, when the conquistador Diego de Ordás climbed it looking for sulphur for gunpowder. For centuries climbers followed, but on 21 December 1994 \"Don Goyo\", as locals call it, erupted after almost half a century of rest, and since then the summit has been closed: the authorities forbid any ascent, and the millions who live within sight of it watch its plume instead.\n\n"],["=",165],["+","\n| Popocatépetl | c. 5,426 m | Mexico | Closed since the 1994 eruption |"]]},{"field":"bodyDe","diff":[["=",106],["-","Fünf"],["+","Sechs"],["=",7],["-","fünf"],["+","sechs"],["=",819],["+","In Mexiko hat der Berg selbst die Antwort gegeben. Der **Popocatépetl**, mit rund 5.426 Metern der zweithöchste Gipfel des Landes, erhebt sich etwa 65 bis 70 Kilometer südöstlich von Mexiko-Stadt; sein Name bedeutet auf Nahuatl „rauchender Berg“, und die Azteken verehrten ihn zusammen mit seinem Schwestervulkan Iztaccíhuatl als heiliges Paar. Die erste überlieferte Besteigung gelang im November 1519 dem Konquistador Diego de Ordás, der Schwefel für Schießpulver suchte. Jahrhundertelang folgten ihm Bergsteiger, doch am 21. Dezember 1994 brach „Don Goyo“, wie die Einheimischen ihn nennen, nach fast einem halben Jahrhundert Ruhe aus, und seitdem ist der Gipfel gesperrt: Die Behörden verbieten jede Besteigung, und die Millionen Menschen, die in Sichtweite leben, beobachten stattdessen seine Rauchfahne.\n\n"],["=",161],["+","\n| Popocatépetl | ca. 5.426 m | Mexiko | Seit dem Ausbruch 1994 gesperrt |"]]}]},
